# Experiment TOML Files

## Off is exact — never approximate

Setting a rate, probability, or weight to `0` (or omitting a field that defaults to `0`) must produce the **identical random stream and controller layout** as if the feature did not exist. This is not a convention; it is a correctness contract enforced by `tests/golden.rs`.

```toml
# ✅ Disabling sensing is exact — no sensor gene drawn, controller unchanged
sensor_probability = 0

# ✅ Disabling joint wear is exact — no caution gene, same input count
joint_endurance = 0

# ❌ Never use a tiny nonzero value to "almost disable" a feature
sensor_probability = 0.001  # draws randomness, changes controller layout
```

## Adding a new experiment

- Copy the closest existing `.toml` and change only what you intend to test.
- Give it a name that describes the independent variable, not the hypothesis (`brittle-walkers.toml`, not `joints-should-fail.toml`).
- After running it, verify the corpse gate: `cargo run --release --example dead_organism_probe -- runs/<your-run>`. Record the result alongside your findings.
- If the dynamics change (new terrain model, new solver setting, etc.) it is a new experiment — do not resume an old run under new settings.

## Changing an existing experiment

Changing any field that affects dynamics (physics, genome, terrain, solver) changes the experiment. The stored runs are no longer comparable to new runs. Note this explicitly if you change a shipped experiment file; consider making a new file instead.

## Fields that affect `config digest`

Any field in `fingerprint()` in `src/config.rs` is included in the resume guard. Changing such a field in a config file will prevent `--resume` from working on existing runs. This is intentional; do not add an escape hatch.
